%9Setting:%9

    window_close_disconnects (boolean; default: ON)

%9Description:%9

    Disconnects a server when /WINDOW CLOSE closes the last window that
    shows it, so a connection you can no longer see is not left open.

    A server is shown by its network window (named after the server tag),
    any window whose server is sticky to it, a window with a channel or a
    query of it, and a window kept for one of its channels (waiting to
    rejoin after a reconnect). A window not bound to any server that shows
    messages, such as the irssi status window, shows every server: the
    messages of a server without a window of its own land there. The
    Notices window and windows without a level (such as Mentions) show no
    server. As long as one window shows the server, it stays connected:
    closing the network window while its channels are open in other
    windows changes nothing, and in a layout with a status window closing
    a channel window never disconnects.

    The server quits with quit_message, as with /DISCONNECT, and a pending
    reconnect of it is dropped. One line in the window that becomes active
    says so. A later /CONNECT gets the server's tag again, not the tag with
    a number appended.

    With the setting OFF a closed window leaves its server connected, as in
    irssi: the connection goes on without a window (/CONNECT to the network
    then only says it is connected, see /HELP CONNECT).

    Only /WINDOW CLOSE does this (also as /WINDOW KILL and the /WC alias,
    for one window or a range): a window closed by /PART or by a query
    ending, /DISCONNECT, /QUIT, /UPGRADE and a lost connection work as
    before.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET window_close_disconnects on
    /SET window_close_disconnects off

%9See also:%9

    SET, WINDOW, CONNECT, DISCONNECT, RMRECONNS, SET quit_message
